Quick note — you wrote two different last-four values (the masked form "***-**-7890" and then "last four: 5678"). Which is the correct last four? I’ll assume that was a typo, but please confirm before you send anything with digits.

High-level guidance
- Treat the full SSN as highly sensitive. Never email an unencrypted scan of the full SSN unless absolutely required and you verify a secure submission method.
- Only provide the full SSN if the form explicitly requires it and the recipient provides a secure channel (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or hand-delivery). Otherwise, ask if the last 4 digits will suffice.
- The last four digits are commonly accepted for identification, but they are still sensitive — only share them when necessary.

Practical redaction and submission tips
1. Confirm what the recipient actually needs
   - Ask the organization: “Do you need the full SSN or will the last four digits be sufficient? What secure method should I use to submit it?”
2. If you do NOT need to send the full SSN
   - Redact (hide) everything except the last four digits.
   - Remove any SSN from the filename (don’t name the file “SSN_123-45-6789.pdf”).
3. If you DO need to send the full SSN, use a secure channel
   - Preferred: their secure upload portal or an encrypted file transfer service.
   - If you must send by email: put the SSN inside a password‑protected PDF and share the password by a separate channel (e.g., text or phone).
4. How to redact correctly (digital)
   - Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat “Redact” tool, PDF editors with a redaction feature). These remove the text from the file rather than just covering it up.
   - After redacting, use “Apply” or “Sanitize” and save as a new file. Verify by searching the PDF for the digits — they shouldn’t be found.
   - If you used an image editor to black out text, flatten the image (export/print to PDF) so no hidden text layers remain.
   - Remove metadata: save a fresh copy, or use the “Remove hidden information”/“Sanitize” option if available.
5. How to redact correctly (paper)
   - Use a permanent black marker to fully cover the SSN, then photocopy or scan the redacted page. Don’t just cover with white-out or place a sticker — those can sometimes be reversed or seen by holding to light.
   - Shred or securely store the original after scanning if not needed.
6. Mobile devices
   - Many mobile mark-up tools only draw over an image layer but can leave underlying searchable text. Prefer an app that explicitly supports redaction (Adobe mobile app, PDF Expert) or scan the paper after marking with a permanent marker.
7. Extra precautions
   - Verify recipient identity and retention policy (how long they keep the document, how it’s stored).
   - Don’t include SSNs in email subject lines or filenames.
   - Keep a record of what you submitted and whom you contacted.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the organization asking whether the last four digits are sufficient and how they want the SSN submitted.
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting on your device (Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android).
- Check the scan you prepared (if you want to tell me how you prepared it) to confirm redaction steps you used.

Which last-four is correct, and how do you plan to send the scan (upload portal, email, or physical delivery)?

