General guidance for sending a scanned form that includes an SSN

- Confirm what the recipient actually needs
  - Ask the organization whether they accept just the last 4 digits instead of the full SSN. Many places accept the last 4 for ID verification.
  - If the full SSN is required, ask for a secure upload option (secure portal), instructions for encrypted delivery, or a mailing address to send a hard copy.

- If you can redact: redact everything except the last 4 digits
  - Redact the full SSN except the last 4 (or redact entirely if last 4 aren’t needed). Never send more of the number than required.
  - Don’t rely on visually “covering” or placing a black box on top of text in a PDF unless the file is flattened or truly redacted — that overlay can sometimes be reversed or the underlying text can still be extracted.

- How to redact safely
  - Easiest physical method: print the form, black out the unwanted digits with a permanent marker, then scan the blacked-out paper. That produces an image that cannot be reversed.
  - Best digital method (recommended if available): use a true redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Sanitize Document). After redaction, save a new file and confirm you cannot select or copy the redacted text.
  - If you must edit in consumer tools: after visually blacking out text in a PDF, “flatten” or print to PDF (this rasterizes the page) and confirm the underlying text is gone. Check by trying to select/copy the redacted area.

- Remove metadata and hidden data
  - After creating the redacted file, remove document metadata or hidden information (Acrobat has “Sanitize” or “Remove Hidden Information”). Some scanners and phone apps embed metadata; clear it if possible.

- Secure transmission
  - Use the recipient’s secure portal when offered.
  - If emailing, password-protect the file (PDF password or encrypted ZIP) and send the password by a different channel (phone call or SMS).
  - Alternatively, use an encrypted email service or a secure file-transfer link (e.g., the organization’s secure upload, OneDrive/Drive with link expiration and password, or a secure file-share service).
  - Avoid sending full SSNs over plain, unencrypted email.

- Keep records and limit storage
  - Don’t keep unsecured copies of the scanned file (delete local copies or store them encrypted).
  - Avoid putting SSNs in email body text or subject lines.

- If the form must include full SSN and you have no secure upload option
  - Ask whether you can deliver it by mail or in person, or whether they can provide a secure upload link.

