Short answer: yes — unless the recipient specifically requires the full SSN, you should redact all but the minimum required (usually the last four). Also avoid sending unencrypted email or attachments containing the full SSN.

One quick note — your message contains two different “last four” values. Which is correct? (I won’t repeat any SSN digits unless you confirm.)

Practical guidance and a step-by-step checklist

1. Confirm what's actually required
- Ask the requester whether they need the full SSN or just the last four digits. Only provide the full number if it is absolutely required and you trust the recipient.

2. If only the last four are needed
- Redact the other digits completely (not just obscure them). A solid black box is standard.

3. How to redact safely (recommended methods)
- Best: Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact). The redaction feature removes underlying text/OCR layers and permanently deletes the data when you “Apply” redactions and save.
- If you must edit an image: open in an image editor, draw a filled black rectangle over the SSN, and export as a new flattened image (PNG/JPEG). Then insert that image into a new PDF and save.
- If you don’t have redaction tools: make a photocopy, redact the copy with a permanent black marker (cover the number thoroughly), then scan the redacted copy. Keep the original secure.
- Important: Don’t just place a black shape over the text in Word/Preview and save — that can leave the underlying searchable text intact. Always flatten or use a true redaction feature, or print-to-PDF after redaction and verify.

4. Remove hidden data and OCR text
- After redaction, sanitize or remove metadata and any hidden text layers. In Acrobat: File → Properties → Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize.
- Or print the redacted file to a new PDF (virtual PDF printer) to flatten layers, then verify you can’t search for the SSN.

5. Secure transmission
- Prefer secure upload portals (employer or government portals), not email.
- If you must email, password-protect the PDF and send the password separately (e.g., password by phone or SMS).
- Consider encrypting the message or using an encrypted file share (e.g., OneDrive/SharePoint with link expiration, Dropbox with password-protected link, or a secure file transfer service).

6. Verify recipient and retention
- Confirm the recipient’s email address and that they need the data.
- Ask how long they will retain the document and how it will be protected.

7. Keep records
- Keep a copy of what you sent and when, and note why the full SSN was provided (if you did provide it). If you redacted, keep the redacted version and a note explaining the redaction.

Quick checklist before sending
- [ ] Confirm only last four (or full SSN) is needed
- [ ] Redact non-required digits using a true redaction method
- [ ] Flatten/sanitize PDF and verify searchable text does not contain the SSN
- [ ] Use secure transmission (portal or encrypted/ password-protected file)
- [ ] Send password separately if used
- [ ] Confirm recipient identity and retention policy

