Best practices and concrete steps

1. Do you need to include the full SSN?
- Only provide the full SSN if the requester explicitly requires the complete number and you trust their secure channel. Often forms or organizations accept just the last 4 digits for identity verification. Ask the recipient whether the last four are sufficient or if they need the full SSN — and request a secure upload method if they do.

2. Redaction guidance (make it permanent)
- Use a proper redaction tool whenever possible (Adobe Acrobat Pro, enterprise PDF redaction tools). These remove the text from the PDF content layer, not just cover it visually.
- If you can’t use a redaction tool, convert the document to a flattened image after obscuring the number:
  - Mark the SSN with a solid black rectangle (not a highlight). Then “flatten” or print-to-PDF / export so the redaction becomes part of the image (no editable layers or hidden text).
  - Confirm there is no searchable OCR text that still contains the SSN (search the PDF for the digits).
- Never “redact” by placing a black box in an editable layer and saving the file without flattening — that can be reversed.

3. How to redact on common devices
- macOS Preview: Use the markup rectangle, fill black; then File > Export as PDF OR Print > Save as PDF to flatten. Then search the PDF for the digits to confirm removal.
- Windows (no Acrobat Pro): Open as an image (Paint, Photos), draw a solid black box, save; then convert to PDF. Or use a reputable PDF editor’s Redact tool. Finally search the saved PDF for the digits.
- iPhone (Notes or Files): Scan > Markup > draw black rectangle > Share > Print > Save as PDF (this flattens). Then open the PDF and try to select/search text for the SSN to ensure it’s not in OCR text.
- Android: Use your phone’s scanner or a trusted scanner app, draw black box, then export/print-to-PDF (or use a redaction-capable PDF app). Verify by searching/exporting text.

4. Verify redaction fully
- Search the file (Ctrl/Cmd+F) for the full SSN and the last four. If it finds anything, the number still exists in text and needs proper removal.
- Check metadata and filename — don’t include the SSN there.
- If possible, open the PDF in a different viewer to confirm the SSN isn’t recoverable.

5. Secure transmission
- Prefer a secure upload portal provided by the institution.
- If you must email:
  - Password-protect the PDF (strong password) and send the password separately (phone call or text).
  - Use encrypted email or a secure file-transfer service. Avoid unencrypted email and avoid public Wi‑Fi.
- Confirm recipient identity before sending. Only share with official addresses/domains you recognize.

6. Retention and cleanup
- After sending, delete intermediate files (scans with full SSN) from your device and any cloud backups if not needed.
- If the recipient doesn’t need the full SSN, ask them to confirm deletion of any copies they don’t require.

Quick checklist before you send
- Confirm whether full SSN is required.
- If not required, send only last four.
- Redact using a proper redaction tool or flatten the image after obscuring.
- Search the file to confirm no SSN text remains.
- Send via secure portal or encrypted/pw-protected PDF; share password separately.
- Delete unneeded copies afterwards.

