Short answer: yes — redact everything except the last four digits unless the recipient specifically requires your full SSN, and never send an unprotected scan of a full SSN over regular email or an insecure channel.

Guidance and concrete steps

1) Before you send: confirm what the recipient actually needs
- If they only need the last four digits, send only the last four (keep the rest redacted).
- If they explicitly require the full SSN (e.g., for payroll, government, tax forms), ask whether they provide a secure upload portal or an encrypted submission method. If they do, use that.

2) Redaction best practices (make the redaction permanent)
- Never just draw a black box or blur in a PDF viewer and send — many annotation layers leave the underlying text accessible.
- For scanned documents (images): permanently edit the image (Paint, Preview, Photos, or an image editor) and place a solid black rectangle over the digits to redact, then save a new file. That produces a new image with the SSN removed.
- For PDFs:
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions, then File → Save As a new PDF. Also run Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize.
  - If you don’t have Acrobat Pro: convert the page to an image, edit the image to black out the digits, then recreate a PDF from the edited image (this “burns in” the blackout).
  - On macOS Preview: add a filled rectangle, then export/print to PDF (or “Export as PDF”); double-check the underlying text is removed. If unsure, convert to image and re-PDF.
- After redaction, open the final file and verify you cannot select/search the redacted digits.

3) Remove metadata
- Images and PDFs may contain metadata (EXIF, author, previous versions). Remove it:
  - Image: use Photos/Preview or “Remove Properties” in Windows file Properties.
  - PDF: use Acrobat’s “Sanitize Document” or a PDF metadata remover.
- If you convert to image and re-PDF, that generally clears most metadata.

4) Secure transmission
- Preferred: upload to the organization’s secure portal or submit through an encrypted web form.
- If you must email:
  - Encrypt the attachment (password-protected PDF or password-protected .zip with AES-256).
  - Share the password through a separate channel (phone call or SMS), not in the same email.
  - Do not put SSN digits in the email subject or message body.
- Tools:
  - Adobe Acrobat: Protect → Encrypt with Password.
  - Microsoft Word: File → Info → Protect Document → Encrypt with Password → Save as PDF.
  - macOS Preview: File → Export as PDF and check “Encrypt” (set password).
  - 7-Zip (Windows): create an AES-256 password-protected archive.
- Confirm the recipient’s email supports secure transport and that the recipient is legitimate.

5) If you must show only last four digits
- Mask like this on the scan: XXX-XX-5678 (or fully redact all but the last four on the document image).
- Ensure the rest of the number is not recoverable from the file or metadata.

6) Extra precautions
- Use a wired or trusted Wi‑Fi network (not public Wi‑Fi) when uploading or sending sensitive docs.
- Keep copies only as long as necessary and store them encrypted if you must keep them.
- If in doubt, call the organization’s support line and confirm the safest submission method.

Quick checklist
