Short answer: In most cases you should avoid sending your full SSN on a scanned document unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure upload channel. If you must send a scan, permanently redact everything except the last four digits (or share only the last four if that suffices) and transmit the file over a secure method.

Practical guidance and steps

1) Decide whether the full SSN is actually required
- Ask the recipient: do they accept just the last 4 digits or a masked form (***-**-5678)? Many organizations only need the last 4 for ID purposes.
- If they insist on the full SSN, request a secure upload portal or encrypted channel (SFTP, secure web portal, or encrypted email).

2) Redaction best practices (do this before sending)
- Use a proper digital redaction tool that removes the underlying text layer (not just draws a black box on top).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Tools → Protect → Remove Hidden Information (Sanitize) → Save as a new file.
  - macOS Preview: Preview’s annotation boxes are not true redaction (they can be removed). Instead, export the page as an image (File → Export → PNG/JPEG), open the image, draw a filled black box over the SSN, then export that image to PDF. Or use a PDF redaction app (PDF Expert, PDFpen) that supports permanent redaction.
  - Windows/free option: Open the PDF/image in a trusted editor (GIMP, Paint.NET) and permanently paint over the digits, then export to PDF. Or use a free PDF redaction tool that explicitly states it “removes” the text.
- After redaction, verify it is permanent: try to select/search for the redacted digits in the saved file—nothing should be selectable or searchable.
- Metadata: remove hidden data and metadata (properties, attachments, layers). Adobe Acrobat has “Remove Hidden Information” / “Sanitize.” If you can’t remove metadata, convert the redacted page to a flattened image/PDF so no text layer or metadata remains.

3) Physical redaction (if scanning a paper copy)
- If you must redact on paper, permanently black out the area with an opaque marker (e.g., black Sharpie), then make a photocopy of the redacted copy and scan the photocopy. Do not just draw a box or sticker and scan the original—transparent ink or scanning settings may reveal underlying text.
- Still preferable to use digital redaction after scanning.

4) File naming and email content
- Don’t include the full SSN (or even last 4 unless necessary) in the file name or email subject. Use neutral names like “ID_form_redacted.pdf.”
- Don’t put the SSN in the email body. If you must share the last 4, write it masked: ***-**-5678.

5) Secure transmission
- Use the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
- If sending by email, use encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or password-protected PDF with a strong password; send the password via a different channel (text/call).
- Use a secure file-share service with expiring links and access controls.

6) Quick verification checklist before sending
- Is full SSN actually required? If not, share only last 4.
- Redaction done with a tool that removes underlying text.
- Confirm you cannot select/search the redacted digits.
- Remove metadata / flatten file.
- File name and subject don’t contain SSN.
- Use a secure upload or encrypt the file in transit.

