General guidance — what to do when you need to send a scanned form that contains an SSN:

1. Confirm what’s actually required
- Ask the requester whether they really need the full SSN or if the last 4 digits will suffice. Many organizations only need the last four for verification.

2. Prefer a secure submission channel
- Best: use the organization’s secure upload portal or encrypted form. Government/tax/HR portals usually provide this.
- Safer alternatives: in-person delivery, secure file-transfer system, or a dedicated secure messaging service.
- Avoid sending unencrypted full SSNs over regular email.

3. If you must send a scan by email, protect the file
- Encrypt the file or use a password-protected PDF and send the password separately (e.g., call or text).
- Use S/MIME, PGP, or the organization’s supported encrypted email option if available.

4. Redact anything not required
- If only the last 4 are needed, permanently redact the other digits before scanning or sending.
- Do NOT just draw a black box in an annotation layer — the underlying text/image can sometimes be recovered.
- Use a proper redaction tool that “applies” the redaction (Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool, PDF redaction features in Preview/third‑party apps) or permanently remove/crop the portion in an editor and save a new flattened file.

5. How to reliably redact/save (quick methods)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save As.
- macOS Preview: Use markup to black out, then Export → PDF and choose “Save as PDF” — but better to use a dedicated redaction tool because Preview annotations can be reversible in some cases. For certainty, after blacking out, Print → Save as PDF (this flattens).
- Windows (no Acrobat): open image in Paint, paint over digits with solid color, Save as new image → then Print to PDF (this flattens). Or use a free PDF editor with a permanent redact feature.
- Mobile: scanning apps (e.g., Adobe Scan) + Acrobat mobile allow redaction and export to flattened PDF. If you only draw on the image, make sure you export/flatten.

6. Verify recipient and purpose
- Confirm the request is legitimate (call the official number or confirm via known contact rather than replying to an unexpected email).
- Check the domain/email address, and avoid clicking suspicious links.

7. After sending (if full SSN shared)
- Keep an audit of what you sent and when.
- Consider placing a fraud alert or credit monitoring if you’re concerned.

If you tell me which device or app you’re using (Windows, macOS, iPhone, Android, Adobe Acrobat, etc.), I can give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for that tool. Also confirm which last-four you meant if you want me to refer to it.

