Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Only share the full SSN when the recipient explicitly requires it and you can transmit it securely. Otherwise redact everything except the last four digits (5678) before scanning/sending, and follow secure-transmission practices.

Guidance and steps

1) Check whether the recipient actually needs the full SSN
- Examples that commonly require the full SSN: tax forms (W‑2, 1099), payroll/hiring background checks, some government agencies, certain credit/loan applications. If in doubt, ask the requester whether the last four digits are sufficient or whether they provide a secure upload method.

2) If the full SSN is NOT required
- Redact everything except the last four digits (5678).
- Use a proper redaction method (see below) so the redacted data cannot be recovered.
- Do not put the SSN (even last four) in the email subject or body if you can avoid it.

3) If the full SSN IS required, transmit securely
- Use the organization’s secure portal/upload link whenever possible.
- If you must email, send a password-protected PDF (strong password) and share the password separately (by phone or text).
- Consider encrypting the email or using secure file transfer (SFTP, secure portal).

4) How to redact correctly (don’t use “hide” or just white-out)
- Best: Use a PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool or similar) that permanently removes the underlying text.
- If you don’t have that: convert the page to a flattened image (scan to image or print-to-PDF after placing a black box), then save as a new PDF — make sure the underlying text layer is removed.
- Avoid simply placing a white rectangle over text in an editable PDF — the original text may still be in the file and recoverable.
- For image scans: blackout with a solid color and then export just the image (not an editable layer). Remove EXIF/metadata.

5) Remove metadata and minimize what you send
- Strip PDF/image metadata or “sanitise” the PDF.
- Scan only the needed pages (don’t include unrelated pages that contain other PII).
- Don’t include SSN in the filename.

6) Additional protections
- Keep a record of who you sent it to and why.
- Delete or securely store copies you don’t need.
- If you must send multiple PII items, ask if they accept a secure upload or direct submission.

If you want, I can:
- Help draft a short message asking the recipient for a secure upload option,
- Walk you through redacting a PDF on your device (tell me whether you’re on Windows or Mac and what software you have),
- Or check whether the recipient type normally requires a full SSN (tell me who/what organization it is).

