Short answer: Yes — you should redact any part of your SSN that the recipient does not explicitly need. Only share the minimum (often just the last four digits) and use a secure method to send it.

Quick guidelines
- Share only what's required. Many forms/organizations only need the last four digits for ID; ask if they truly need the full SSN.
- Prefer secure portals. If the organization offers a secure upload site or portal, use that instead of regular email.
- If you must send a scanned document, permanently remove the SSN from the file (don’t just hide it visually).

How to redact properly (digital scans / PDFs)
1. Use a real redaction tool (best): Adobe Acrobat Pro, or another PDF editor with a redaction feature. Redaction tools remove the text and metadata so it can’t be recovered.
   - In Acrobat: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save As a new file.
2. If you don’t have Acrobat, convert the page to an image, edit it, then re-save:
   - Open the scan in an image editor (Preview on Mac, Paint/photos app on Windows, or any image editor).
   - Use a solid black box or erase the SSN area (not just blur). Save as a new image or export to PDF.
   - Confirm the removed area contains no text layer or hidden metadata.
3. If scanning a physical paper, you can cover the digits with opaque tape or a black marker on the paper before scanning — then scan that version. (But ensure the underlying text truly isn’t visible in the resulting file.)
4. After redaction, verify by opening the file and trying to select/copy the area — nothing should be selectable or recoverable.

Secure sending tips
- Use the organization’s secure upload portal whenever possible.
- If emailing is the only option, password-protect the PDF and send the password by a separate channel (e.g., text or phone).
- Don’t put SSN digits in the file name or the email subject/body.
- Confirm the recipient’s email address and that they request SSN by email. If in doubt, call them to confirm the secure method.
- Consider alternatives: giving full SSN by phone, in person, or via a verified secure form.

Other precautions
- Keep a copy of what you sent, but store it encrypted or in a secure location.
- Delete temporary files and empty the recycle/trash after saving the redacted version.
- If the organization insists on the full SSN and no secure channel exists, request an alternative verification method or ask for a secure fax number.

