Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Only include the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it. If they only need the “last four” (5678), redact the first five digits (***-**-5678) before scanning/sending. If they do require the full number, send it only by a secure channel.

Practical checklist and steps

1) Confirm what’s required
- Ask the recipient: “Do you need the full SSN, or is the last four sufficient? If you need the full SSN, what secure upload/transmission method do you want me to use?”

2) Redaction best practices (do this before scanning or before sending the file)
- Recommended (digital, permanent): Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply) — this permanently removes the underlying text and metadata.
- Free / alternative digital method: Convert the page to an image (scan → save as image), use an image editor to black out the digits, then save/print to PDF. Make sure the PDF is flattened so there’s no hidden text layer.
- Physical method (if you must): Black out with an opaque marker, then photocopy the redacted page and scan the photocopy. Don’t just place a black box in a PDF viewer — that can leave the text underneath.
- Verify: After redacting, open the saved file and try to select or search the redacted digits. If you can highlight or find them, the redaction wasn’t done securely.

3) Remove ancillary risks
- Don’t put SSN digits in the file name, email subject, or email body.
- Remove or sanitize metadata (document properties) where possible.
- Keep other PII to a minimum in the document if not required.

4) Secure transmission options
- Best: secure upload portal provided by the recipient (use that when available).
- Next-best: send a password-protected PDF (use a strong password; AES-256 if offered) and share the password separately (by phone or an SMS/call; avoid sending it in the same email).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or similar) or an encrypted file-sharing link with expiration and access controls.
- Avoid sending unencrypted SSNs over regular email.

5) If you must send the full SSN
- Confirm the requirement in writing.
- Use one of the secure transmission methods above.
- Keep a copy of confirmation and a record of how you sent it.

Sample message to ask the recipient
- “Quick question: for the attached form do you need the full SSN or is the last four digits sufficient? If the full SSN is required, what secure method would you prefer for me to transmit it (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected PDF)?”

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft that message for you to send.
- Walk you through redacting a scan (tell me whether you’re on Windows or macOS and which software you have).

