What to do before sending a scan
1. Prefer alternatives
   - Ask whether the recipient will accept only the last four digits (this is common).
   - If they need the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or an encrypted channel.

2. If you only need to show the last four
   - Redact the first five digits so the file shows only XXX-XX-5678 (or “last 4: 5678”).
   - If possible, provide the last four as plain text in the email body (not the full SSN) rather than embedding the whole SSN in the scan.

3. If the recipient requires the full SSN
   - Use a secure method: a secure portal, an encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP), or a password-protected PDF/ZIP sent by email with the password given separately (SMS or phone).
   - Avoid sending the full SSN over unencrypted email.

How to redact properly (avoid mistakes that leave data recoverable)
- Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool or similar) that removes the text from the document, not just covers it visually.
- For images/photos:
  - Use an image editor to draw a filled black box over the digits, then “flatten” or export to a new file (JPEG/PNG). Open the new file and zoom in to confirm pixels are gone.
- For PDFs created from scans with a text/OCR layer:
  - Use a PDF redaction feature that removes both the visible text and underlying text layer, then save as a new file.
  - After redaction, search the PDF for the SSN to confirm it is not present.
- Never “white out” or place an annotation box and save — annotations/layers can often be removed to reveal the original.
- If you redact by covering the paper before scanning, make sure the cover is opaque so the scanner does not capture the underlying numbers.

Remove extras and metadata
- Strip metadata and comments from the file (PDF properties, camera EXIF for photos).
- Save the redacted file under a new filename.
- Delete the original unredacted file and empty your trash/backups if you no longer need it.

If you must send by email
- Use the least-exposing option: attach only the redacted copy (last four visible) and include a brief note in the email body.
- Verify the recipient’s email address, avoid CC/BCC unless necessary, and request confirmation of receipt.
- If sending a password-protected file, transmit the password through a separate channel (SMS or a phone call).

Quick checklist
