I can help. Quick note first — the masked number you wrote ("***-**-7890") and the parenthetical "last four: 5678" don’t match. Which last four should be visible on the scan? (You don’t need to repeat the full SSN.)

Basic rules
- If the recipient requires the full SSN (e.g., for tax, payroll, background checks), do NOT redact — send it only by a secure method they specify.
- If the form only needs the last four digits, redact or remove the other five digits before sending.
- Avoid sending full SSNs by regular email or unsecured file transfer whenever possible.

How to redact safely (options)
1. Physical redaction before scanning
   - Use a permanent black marker to fully cover the digits you want hidden, let it dry, then scan or photograph the page.
   - Scan the marked page (not the original unmarked file). Verify the covered digits cannot be seen by holding it up to light or zooming.

2. Digital redaction (recommended for best assurance)
   - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply). This removes the text from the file permanently.
   - If you don’t have Acrobat, convert the page to an image (or screenshot), draw a solid black rectangle over the digits, then “flatten” or export to a new PDF/image so the underlying text is gone.
   - On phones: use Markup to black-out, then export/save as an image (not as an editable PDF) to ensure it’s flattened.

3. Things to avoid
   - Don’t just place a white box over text in an editable PDF without flattening — the underlying text may still be recoverable.
   - Don’t use non-permanent ink or removable stickers.
   - Don’t rely on simple “erase” in a PDF editor unless you use a real redaction feature.

4. Check the redaction
   - Try selecting or copying the area with the redaction. If text can be selected/copied, the redaction is not secure.
   - Inspect file properties and remove metadata (EXIF for images, hidden data in PDFs). Many PDF editors have a “Remove Hidden Information” or “Sanitize” feature.

5. Send securely
   - Use the recipient’s secure upload portal or encrypted email if available.
   - If you must email, consider password-protecting the PDF and sending the password via a different channel (e.g., SMS or phone).
   - Confirm with the recipient what they accept before sending a redacted copy.

