Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share what’s necessary. If the recipient only needs the last four digits (5678), redact the rest before sending. If they explicitly require the full SSN, don’t send it over plain email — use a secure portal or an encrypted/ password‑protected file.

Practical guidance and checklist

1) When to redact
- If a form/scanner/email asks for “last 4” or you can confirm they only need the last four, redact the first five digits and send only the last four.
- If the full SSN is required, use a secure channel (see below) rather than unencrypted email.

2) How to redact a scanned document (securely)
- Best: redact before scanning — cover the first five digits on the paper with opaque tape/marker, then scan. Keep an unredacted original in a safe place if you need it.
- Proper digital redaction (recommended): use a tool that actually removes underlying text/image data (Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool, PDF Redact Tools, etc.). Steps in Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save As. Then run “Remove Hidden Information/Inspect Document.”
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: rasterize the page (convert PDF to an image) then paint a solid black box over the digits, then save that as a new PDF. Make sure the file is flattened so there’s no hidden text/OCR layer underneath.
- Don’t rely on blur/soft pixelation — these can sometimes be reversed. Don’t use white boxes that might be removed; use a solid black box and flatten/rasterize.

3) Confirm redaction worked
- After saving, open the file and try to search for those digits (or copy/paste text) to confirm they’re gone.
- If possible, have someone you trust verify the redaction without seeing the unredacted original.

4) Secure sending options
- Preferred: upload to the recipient’s secure portal (employer/government agency) — this is the safest.
- If you must email: send a password‑protected PDF (AES‑256 if available) and send the password over a separate channel (phone or SMS). Or use an encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or an encrypted file‑sharing link with expiration and access controls.
- Verify recipient email address and legitimacy before sending sensitive data.

5) Other tips
- Redact any other unneeded PII (DOB, driver’s license, account numbers).
- Keep an unredacted original stored securely.
- Ask the requester: “Do you need the full SSN or will the last four suffice?” — this avoids unnecessary exposure.

If you want, tell me:
- Who you’re sending it to (employer, government agency, bank), and
- What device/software you have (Adobe Acrobat, Mac Preview, Windows tools, phone).

I can give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for your specific platform or draft a short message asking the recipient whether the last four will be enough.

