General guidance (what you should consider before sending a scanned form)
- Only share the full SSN when it’s absolutely required by the recipient and for a legitimate purpose (tax forms, payroll, government ID verification, etc.). Ask the requester whether they really need the full 9 digits or if the last 4 will suffice.
- Use secure channels: prefer an official secure upload portal (HTTPS) or encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) over plain email. If none are available, require a password‑protected file and send the password by a separate channel (phone/SMS).
- Minimize exposure: don’t include SSN (even last 4) in the email subject line or filename.

How to redact a scanned document properly (so metadata and underlying text aren’t recoverable)
1. Work from a copy — keep an original stored securely, redact a copy for transmission.
2. Use a true redaction tool (recommended):
   - PDF: Use Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool (Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save as a new file). This permanently removes the redacted content and the underlying text layer.
   - Other PDF tools: many PDF editors (PDF-XChange, Nitro PDF, Preview on Mac has limited redaction — be cautious) offer a “Redact” or “Sanitize” feature. Look for “apply redaction” or “sanitize document.”
3. If you only have an image:
   - Open the scan in an image editor, paint over the SSN with a solid color (black) at the pixel level, then export/save as a new flattened image (e.g., JPG or PNG). Do not leave layers that can be removed.
   - Convert that flattened image into a PDF if needed.
4. Avoid ineffective redaction:
   - Don’t redact by placing a black box layer in a PDF viewer without applying/finalizing it — the underlying text can remain and be extracted with OCR or copy/paste.
   - Don’t just white‑out on the printed paper and scan again unless you’re sure the white‑out fully hides it; better to redact digitally.
5. Remove metadata:
   - After redaction, use the PDF editor’s “Remove hidden information” or “Sanitize” tool to strip metadata and hidden layers.
   - For images, strip EXIF data (many image viewers have “remove properties” or use an online tool — but prefer offline tools for privacy).
6. Password protection and delivery:
   - If sending by email, encrypt the attachment (AES‑256 if available) or password‑protect the PDF and share the password via phone/SMS.
   - Prefer uploading to the requester’s secure portal. Verify the portal URL is legitimate and uses HTTPS.
7. Verify recipient and need:
   - Call or message the organization to confirm their recommended secure submission method before sending.
   - Ask if they accept a redacted copy (first five digits redacted, only last four visible) if full SSN isn’t strictly required.

If the form requires only the last four
- Redact the first five digits permanently (as above) and leave only the last four visible. Confirm the redaction is irreversible (use a proper redaction tool).

