Short answer: Yes—if your scan will show the full SSN you should redact everything except the last four digits unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number. When a full SSN is required, send it only by a secure method (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file with password sent separately).

What to do (practical steps and why):

1. Confirm what’s needed
- Ask the recipient whether they truly need the full SSN or only the last 4 digits. If they only need the last four, send a scan with the first five digits redacted.

2. Redact correctly (don’t just cover with an annotation)
- Visual overlays (white boxes, black rectangles drawn as annotations) often only hide the characters visually but leave the underlying text/selectable text or metadata intact.
- Use a proper redaction feature that “applies” redactions and removes underlying text and metadata. After redaction, try to select or search the document to confirm the SSN is gone.

3. How to redact depending on what you have
- If you have Adobe Acrobat Pro:
  - Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select SSN) → Apply Redactions → Sanitize Document (remove hidden info) → Save as a new file.
- If you only have an image (photo/scan saved as JPG/PNG):
  - Black out the digits in an image editor (make it permanent pixels), save a new image, then convert to PDF if needed. Check with an OCR/text selection tool to be sure digits aren’t recoverable.
- If you use macOS Preview or a basic PDF viewer without a true redaction tool:
  - Best to convert the page to an image (File → Export as PNG/JPEG), edit the image to black out digits, then save/convert back to PDF. Or use a trusted redaction app (PDF Expert, PDFpen) or an online redaction service you trust.
- If using online tools: be careful — only use reputable services and avoid uploading highly sensitive documents to unknown sites.

4. Remove metadata and hidden content
- After redacting, remove metadata/hidden text (Acrobat’s “Sanitize Document” or “Remove Hidden Information”).
- If you used an image route, strip EXIF/metadata (many image editors and “save for web” options remove it; there are metadata removal tools too).

5. Secure transmission
- Prefer: secure upload portal the recipient provides, or encrypted email/secure file transfer.
- If you must email: password‑protect the PDF (AES encryption) and send the password separately (SMS or phone call).
- Avoid sending unprotected SSN scans over plain email or public file-sharing links.

6. Keep originals and copies secure
- Keep the original (unredacted) file in a safe place and delete local copies you don’t need. Note that redaction is irreversible on the redacted file; keep a secure backup if you’ll need the full copy later.

Quick checklist before sending:
- Confirm recipient needs full SSN or only last 4.
- Use a real redaction tool (not just a visual cover).
- Sanitize/remove metadata.
- Send via secure method or password-protect and share password separately.
- Verify the redaction by attempting to select/search the text.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether they need the full SSN or a secure upload method, or
- Give step‑by‑step redaction instructions tailored to your device (Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android) and the software you have. Which would you prefer?

