Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only send the full SSN if the recipient absolutely requires it and you use a secure transfer method. Otherwise redact everything except the last four (5678) before sending a scan, and prefer a secure upload or encrypted file instead of plain email.

Detailed, practical guidance

1) First — confirm whether the recipient really needs the full SSN
- Many forms only need the last four digits. Ask the requester which form fields are mandatory and whether a masked SSN (e.g., ***-**-5678) is acceptable.
- If the full SSN is required (for payroll, tax, background check, government benefit enrollment, etc.), confirm they provide a secure upload portal or secure transmission instructions.

2) If you can redact (recommended)
- Redact the entire SSN except the last four (or redact all digits if they don’t need any): leave only 5678 visible.
- Don’t put the SSN in the email subject line or filename.

3) How to redact correctly (avoid “fake” redaction)
- For PDFs created by a scanner or from a digital document:
  - Use a proper redaction tool that actually removes underlying text and metadata (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions).
  - Do NOT just draw a black box annotation or white-out on top of the text in a PDF without applying redaction — underlying text can still be copied or recovered.
- For image scans (JPEG/PNG) or if you scan after physically redacting:
  - Physically blackout the number with an opaque marker before scanning (this is safe).
  - Or edit the image in an image editor, put a black box over the SSN, then export/flatten and save as a new image file (this removes underlying text).
- After redacting, verify:
  - Open the saved file and try selecting/copying the just-redacted region to make sure digits can’t be recovered.
  - Search the document for the digits to confirm they aren’t present in text or metadata.

4) Remove metadata
- Some files contain metadata (author, comments) that might include sensitive details. Use your PDF editor to remove metadata or “Save as” a new, flattened file.

5) Secure transmission
- Best: upload to the recipient’s secure portal or use an encrypted file-transfer service.
- If you must email:
  - Password-protect the PDF with strong encryption (AES-256) and send the password by a different channel (text or phone call).
  - Or use end-to-end encrypted email or a secure file-share (OneDrive/SharePoint link with expiration and access controls, Dropbox with password-protected link, Proton Drive, etc.).
- Avoid sending unencrypted attachments with full SSN over regular email/TLS if you can avoid it.

6) If you must send the full SSN
- Verify recipient identity and that they have a secure method.
- Use password-protected/encrypted files, secure portals, or encrypted email.
- Include a short cover note stating why the full SSN is provided and request confirmation they will delete or securely store the copy when no longer needed.

7) Retention and follow-up
- Ask the recipient to confirm secure receipt.
- If not required to keep a copy, delete the sent copy from your device and email “Sent” folder or store it securely per best practices.

If you’d like, I can:
- Walk you step-by-step through redaction on your device (Windows/Mac/phone) — tell me which OS/app you have.
- Draft a short message to the requester asking whether the last four digits are sufficient or requesting their secure upload method.

