Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually you should NOT include your full SSN in an ordinary email or an unencrypted scan. Only share the full number when absolutely required and then use a secure method; otherwise provide just the last four digits (or redact the first five).

Key points and recommended steps

1. Confirm what's required
- Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last four digits will suffice. Many organizations accept last-four only.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload link, encrypted email, or a secure portal rather than regular email or text.

2. Prefer redaction when possible
- If the form or recipient only needs proof of identity, provide the last four digits and redact the rest.
- Redact the first five digits so the document shows ***-**-5678 (or simply “last 4: 5678”) rather than the full number.

3. How to redact safely (digital files)
- Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro, PDF editors with a “Redact” feature). Mark the text for redaction and then apply/commit redaction. This removes the underlying text permanently.
- Do NOT just place a black box or white rectangle over the number in a PDF or image without committing the redaction — that may leave the underlying text intact and recoverable.
- After redaction, save as a new file and verify the redacted area cannot be selected or copied.
- Remove file metadata (Document Properties / Remove Hidden Information in Acrobat) before sending.

4. How to redact safely (paper documents)
- Best: request a secure portal so you can scan the document after redacting.
- If you must redact on paper: cover the digits with an opaque adhesive label or use a permanent black marker that completely obscures the numbers, then scan the covered page. Verify the scanned image shows the numbers fully obscured.
- After scanning, check the image zoomed in to be sure nothing remains visible.
- If you used a marker, it’s safer to place a label over the numbers and then scan.

5. Transfer method and other precautions
- Use secure upload portals or encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or a password-protected PDF sent by a separate channel (e.g., send password by phone/SMS).
- Avoid sending SSN in the subject line or in the filename. Use a neutral file name (e.g., ID_document_redacted.pdf).
- Store or keep only what’s necessary. Delete local copies securely when no longer needed.
- If sending to an organization, ask how they handle and store SSNs (retention, encryption).

6. Verify after sending
- Confirm receipt and that the recipient can view the redacted document.
- Ask them to confirm whether they need any additional info and whether they will retain or securely delete the copy you provided.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the requester asking whether full SSN is required and asking for a secure upload link.
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting the document on your phone, Windows PC, or Mac — tell me which device and PDF/image software you have.

