Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually you should avoid sending the full SSN in an email or an unsecured scan. Redact everything except the last four digits unless the recipient specifically requires the full SSN and provides a secure way to send it.

Practical guidance

1. Decide whether the recipient truly needs the full SSN
- Many organizations only need the last 4 digits for ID. Ask the requester: “Do you need the full SSN, or will ‘SSN ending in 5678’ suffice?” If they require the full number, ask for a secure upload portal, encrypted email instructions, or a secure fax number.

2. Redaction best practices
- Keep last 4 only when acceptable: use the form text “SSN ending in 5678” rather than showing the full number.
- Never put the full SSN in the email subject line, filename, or visible body text unless using an approved secure channel.

3. How to redact safely (digital)
- Best: use a proper PDF redaction tool that removes the underlying text (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply; also use “Sanitize Document” to remove hidden data).
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: convert the page to an image and permanently remove the digits in an image editor (paint, Preview, or similar), then save that image as a new PDF. That ensures there’s no hidden text layer left.
- Don’t just place a black/white rectangle on top of text in a PDF viewer that leaves the original text underneath — that can be recovered.

4. Remove metadata
- After redaction, remove or sanitize document metadata/hidden info (Adobe: Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize; other tools may offer similar options).

5. Transport securely
- Preferred: use the organization’s secure upload portal, secure messaging system, or an encrypted email service.
- If you must send by email: password-protect the PDF (strong password) and send the password by a separate channel (text or phone call). On macOS, Preview→Export as PDF and check “Encrypt” (or use Acrobat or other tools). Avoid sending password in the same email.
- Alternatively, compress to an encrypted ZIP (with a strong password) and send the password separately.

6. Verification and record
- Confirm the recipient and confirm that you’ve used their preferred secure channel.
- Keep an original copy for your records and the redacted copy you sent.

Sample wording you can use in an email (if sending a redacted scan)
- Subject: Completed form (SSN redacted)
- Body: “Attached is the completed form. I have redacted the SSN to show only the last four digits: 5678. Please confirm whether you need the full SSN and, if so, provide a secure upload link or instructions for submitting it securely. Thank you.”

If the recipient insists on the full SSN and provides no secure method, consider alternative delivery (secure fax, in-person, or certified mail).

Would you like me to:
- Walk you through redacting a specific file on your computer or phone?
- Create a password-protected PDF (I can give step-by-step instructions for your OS)?
- Draft an email to the requester asking whether the last four digits are sufficient and requesting a secure upload link?

