Quick note before advice: I see the masked SSN you wrote (***-**-7890) and the “last four: 5678” — those don’t match. Which last four is correct? (You don’t need to type the whole number here if you’d rather not.)

Short answer
- Only share your full SSN when the recipient explicitly requires it and they provide a secure method (secure portal, encrypted upload).  
- If the form only needs the last four digits, send only the last four and redact/hide the rest. Never put the full SSN in an email body or subject line unless the channel is secure and requested.

What to redact when sending a scan
- Redact the full SSN except the portion actually required by the requester (usually last 4). If they asked for “last four,” show only the last four.  
- Also consider redacting any other unnecessary PII on the same page (e.g., driver’s license number, passport number) if it’s not required.

How to redact safely (step-by-step)
1. Confirm requirements: Ask the requester whether they need the full SSN or just the last four and what secure submission methods they accept.  
2. Use a proper redaction tool (do not just draw a white box):
   - PDF editor with a Redact tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro, Preview on mac for simple cases) — use the official “redact” feature so the text is removed permanently.
   - If you only have an image, use an image editor to paint an opaque black box over the digits, then export a flattened image/PDF.  
3. Make sure redaction is permanent:
   - After redacting, try copying/searching the document for the SSN digits — if the digits can still be copied or found, the redaction is not permanent.
   - If using a PDF editor, use “Sanitize” or “Remove hidden information” to clear metadata and hidden text layers.  
4. Remove metadata and OCR layers:
   - Re-save the redacted file as a new PDF (sometimes “Print to PDF” or “Export as PDF” helps remove hidden text).  
5. Protect the file in transit:
   - Use the recipient’s secure upload portal when available (preferred).  
   - If you must email, password-protect the PDF with a strong password and send the password by a separate channel (phone call or SMS). Better: use end-to-end encrypted email or a secure file-sharing service with link expiry and access controls.  
6. Keep minimal copies:
   - Keep the original unredacted file only if you must; store it securely (encrypted) and delete any unnecessary copies once the transaction is complete.

Other practical tips
- Don’t include SSNs in subject lines or email bodies.  
- Ask the recipient for their data-handling policy if you’re unsure.  
- If this is for tax, employment, or background check purposes they often require the full SSN — in those cases insist on a secure upload or direct entry into their form/portal.  
- When in doubt, call the organization and confirm their preferred secure submission method.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the requester asking how they want you to submit the SSN securely, or
- Walk you through redacting a specific scanned file (tell me what device/software you have).

