Yes — good question. Short answer: if you can avoid sending the full SSN, do so. Only share the full number when absolutely required and only over a secure channel. Below are practical redaction and sending recommendations.

When to redact
- If the recipient only needs identity verification, the last 4 digits are usually sufficient — redact the rest.  
- If the form specifically requires the full SSN, don’t send it by plain email; follow the official secure submission instructions (secure portal, encrypted upload, fax, or in-person).

How to redact safely (scanned documents)
- Use a true redaction tool (recommended): e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro — Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply → Save a new file. True redaction removes underlying text and metadata.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: convert the page to an image (JPEG/PNG), paint a solid black box over the SSN area, then save that image and create a new PDF from the image. Verify you cannot select/copy the text.
- Avoid simply placing a black shape or white box over text in a PDF without flattening — underlying selectable text or metadata can remain and be recovered.
- After redaction, check: try selecting the SSN text or running OCR — it should not be selectable or readable. Also remove the SSN from the filename and from the email subject/body.

Secure transmission options
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal (best). Many employers/financial institutions provide one.
- If portal not available: password-protect the PDF (strong password) or put the file in a password‑protected ZIP; send the file by email and give the password separately (phone call or SMS).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or other) is acceptable if both sides support it.
- Fax or in-person delivery are also secure alternatives when available.

