Best practices for sending a scanned document that contains an SSN
- Only include the SSN if absolutely required. Ask the recipient first whether they need the full SSN, or if the last 4 digits will suffice.
- Prefer secure upload portals over email. If the organization has a secure portal or encrypted upload, use that.
- If you must send a scan or PDF by email, redact everything except the minimum required (ideally nothing, or at most the last 4 digits).
- Use proper redaction tools — do NOT just place a white box over the numbers:
  - For PDFs: use a PDF editor’s Redact/Sanitize feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark → Apply Redactions → Save). Proper redaction removes the underlying text layer.
  - For images/photos: permanently blackout the digits with an image editor (paint/markup) and save/export so the changes are flattened. Then convert to PDF if needed.
  - For Word docs: replace the SSN text with masked characters (e.g., XXX-XX-1234) before exporting to PDF. If you overlay a black box, make sure to "save as flattened PDF" so the underlying text is gone.
- After redaction, verify: open the resulting file and try to select/copy the redacted area — if you can select the numbers, the redaction was not secure.
- Remove metadata/EXIF:
  - Photos: strip EXIF (many phones/apps can remove metadata or use “Save for web”/export options). On Windows: File → Properties → Details → Remove Properties. On Mac Preview: Tools → Show Inspector → remove info.
  - PDFs/Docs: use “Remove hidden information” / “Sanitize” tools in your editor.
- Encrypt the file or password-protect the PDF, and send the password separately (e.g., by phone or SMS).
- Keep a secure copy of the original and the redacted copy, and document who you sent it to.

Quick step-by-step examples
- Adobe Acrobat (PDF):
  1. Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select numbers) → Apply Redactions.
  2. File → Save As (new name).
  3. Tools → Protect → Encrypt (add password) if sending by email.
- Mac Preview (image/PDF):
  1. Use Markup to draw a black rectangle over SSN.
  2. File → Export as PDF. To be extra safe, print to PDF to flatten.
  3. Remove metadata via an EXIF-removal app if it was a photo.
- Mobile (iPhone/Android):
