Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure method. If you’re sending a scan by email or message, redact everything except the last 4 digits (5678) unless the recipient says they need the full number and offers a secure upload/encryption.

Guidance and practical steps

1) Should you redact?
- If the form only needs “last 4” for ID verification: redact the first five digits and include only the last four (5678).
- If the organization requires the full SSN on the scanned form (some government / tax forms do), do NOT send the scan by unencrypted email. Use their secure upload portal, an encrypted transfer, or send the paper form by secure mail.
- When in doubt, ask the requester: “Do you need the full SSN on the scan, or will the last four digits suffice? If you need the full SSN, what secure upload method should I use?”

2) How to redact correctly (don’t just draw a black box over visible text)
- Use a proper redaction tool that permanently removes the underlying text (Adobe Acrobat Pro, Foxit, PDF-XChange etc.): mark text to redact → Apply Redactions → Save as a new file.
- If you don’t have a PDF redaction tool, convert the page to a flat image (rasterize) and then crop or paint over the digits and save. Make sure the result is flattened (no hidden text/OCR).
- If you use macOS Preview: add a rectangle over the SSN, then export as PDF and flatten the file (print → save as PDF) to remove annotations; however Acrobat’s redaction is safer.
- On phones: use a scanner app that lets you redact and export a flattened PDF, or export as an image and paint over the digits before saving.
- After redaction, verify by selecting text or running a quick OCR search — the redacted digits should not be selectable/searchable.

3) Remove metadata/hidden data
- After redaction, sanitize the file: remove metadata, hidden layers, attachments, and OCR text. Many PDF tools have a “Sanitize” or “Remove hidden information” function.
- If unsure, export just the page(s) you need as a new flattened PDF or image.

4) Secure transmission
- Prefer the organization’s secure upload portal.
- If you must email, password-protect the PDF (AES-256 if available) and send the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS).
- Alternatively, use an encrypted file-sharing service (end-to-end) and a one-time link.
- Avoid sending SSNs in plain text in subject lines, filenames, or email body.

5) Quick checklist before sending
- Do they require full SSN or last 4 only? (If not required, redact to last 4.)
- Is the file properly redacted and flattened (redacted text not selectable)?
- Did you remove metadata and hidden text?
- Is the transfer method secure (portal, encrypted email, passworded PDF)?
- Is the filename free of SSN or sensitive info?

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to ask the requester whether the last four digits are sufficient and what secure method they prefer.
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting the file on your device (tell me whether you’re on Windows, macOS, iPhone, or Android).

