Short answer: only share the full SSN if absolutely required — otherwise redact everything except the last four. Also: I noticed a mismatch in what you typed (masked shows “…7890” but you wrote “last four: 5678”). Which last four is correct?

Practical guidance and steps

- Principle
  - Never include the full SSN in an unencrypted email or an unprotected scan unless the recipient explicitly requires it and you have a safe transfer method. Many organizations accept just the last four digits for verification.

- Before you send: confirm with the recipient
  - Ask whether they actually need the full SSN or whether the last four will do.
  - Ask for a secure upload option (secure portal, encrypted file upload) if they insist on the full SSN.

- Safe ways to share the full SSN (only if required)
  - Use the recipient’s secure portal or a secure file-transfer service.
  - If you must email, password-protect/encrypt the PDF (AES-256) and send the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS).
  - Use encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) if both parties support it.

- How to redact correctly (don’t just hide digits visually)
  - On paper: permanently black out the SSN with a marker, then photocopy or scan the redacted paper. Scanning an already-redacted paper is simple and reliable.
  - Digital PDF (recommended if working electronically): use a proper redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool) which permanently removes the text. After redaction, save a new file and check there’s no selectable/searchable SSN remaining and remove hidden metadata.
  - If the document is an image/scan: permanently edit the image pixels (crop or erase the digits) or use an image editor and save a new file. Don’t just place a black box on top in a layered editor — underlying text may still be present.
  - If you only need to show the last four, redact (remove) all preceding digits and verify by trying to select/search text in the saved file.

- Verify after redaction
  - Open the saved file and try to select/search the SSN. If digits are selectable or searchable, the redaction wasn’t permanent.
  - Optionally run a quick metadata check to ensure no hidden copies remain.

- Minimize exposure
  - Only send the minimum required information.
  - Delete or securely store versions that contain the full SSN once the transaction is complete.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether a secure upload or last-four-only is acceptable.
- Provide step-by-step redaction instructions for Adobe Acrobat, Preview (Mac), or a common free tool — tell me which you use.
- Walk you through verifying the redaction.

Also: please confirm which last-four digits are correct (5678 or 7890) so I don’t repeat the wrong one.

