I can help — a couple quick clarifying points first:

1) You wrote "***-**-7890 (last four: 5678)". Which is the correct last-four you mean to send: 7890 or 5678? (I won't repeat the full number.)

General guidance — when to redact vs when not to
- If the organization explicitly requires the full SSN on the completed form (e.g., tax, payroll, background-check forms), do not redact the SSN on the form itself — they need it to process. Instead, ensure you transmit the scan securely (see secure-transmission steps below).
- If the recipient only needs the last four digits (very common for ID verification), redact everything except the last four before you send.
- If you are not sure what they need, ask the recipient which digits they require and how they prefer you send them (secure portal, encrypted email, etc.) before sending.

How to redact safely (so numbers are actually removed)
- Use a real redaction tool (recommended): Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool that permanently removes selected content and can also sanitize hidden data/metadata.
- If you don't have Acrobat Pro:
  - Convert the page to a flattened image or a new PDF (print to PDF / export to PDF) and then draw solid black boxes over the SSN. After that, flatten/save/export so the underlying text isn’t still present.
  - Another safe method: take a photo of the completed form with the SSN blacked out on paper, then send that photo/PDF.
- Do NOT just put a white rectangle over the numbers in an editable PDF and save — the text can still be recovered unless the file is flattened/redacted.
- After redaction, search the PDF/text layer for the SSN to confirm it’s gone.

Other privacy steps before sending
- Remove metadata/hidden data: use “Sanitize” / “Remove hidden information” in Acrobat or use a metadata-cleaning tool.
- Don’t put the SSN in the file name.
- Password-protect or encrypt the file (PDF password, encrypted ZIP, or use your organization’s secure upload portal). If you password-protect, send the password by a different channel (SMS or phone).
- If the recipient offers a secure portal or an encrypted upload link, prefer that over email attachments.
- Keep a secure copy for your records; delete any insecure transitory copies you don’t need.

Quick checklist
1. Confirm whether full SSN is required.  
2. If only last 4 required → redact the rest using a proper redaction or flattening method.  
3. Remove metadata and check text layer for SSN.  
4. Encrypt/password-protect file or use recipient’s secure portal.  
5. Send password separately (not in same email).  
6. Avoid SSN in filename and delete any unnecessary temporary files.

If you tell me:
- Which last-four is correct (7890 or 5678), and
- What device/software you have (Windows, macOS, iPhone/Android, Acrobat Pro, Preview, etc.),

I can give step‑by‑step instructions for redacting and creating a secure PDF, or draft a short message you can send to the recipient asking how they want the SSN transmitted.

