Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes—redact everything except the minimum the requester explicitly needs (usually just the last 4). Never send the full SSN in an unencrypted email or in a scan you haven’t permanently redacted and secured.

Practical guidance and step-by-step options

1) Confirm what the requester actually needs
- If they only asked for the “last 4,” provide only the last four digits.
- If they explicitly require the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (secure portal, encrypted email, or other approved channel) before sending.

2) Redacting a paper form before scanning (recommended if you already have paper)
- Use a permanent black marker to block the first five digits (do not use removable tape or pencil).
- Let the ink dry, then scan.
- Verify the scanned image: try adjusting contrast/brightness and zoom in to ensure blocked digits are not recoverable.

3) Redacting a digital/PDF scan (recommended for best control)
- Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact”, Preview on Mac with the Redact function, or a PDF editor that supports “Apply Redactions”).
  - Select the characters/area to redact, apply the redaction (this removes underlying text, not just covers it).
  - Save a new file.
- If you only have image editing tools: permanently erase the digits by editing pixels (paint them out) and then export to a flattened image or PDF. Do NOT just draw a black box on top of selectable text—the underlying text can remain.
- After redaction, remove hidden data/metadata:
  - In Acrobat: use “Sanitize Document” / Remove hidden information.
  - Or print to a new PDF (virtual printer) to flatten the file, then check.

4) Verify redaction cannot be undone
- Try selecting/copying the redacted area—there should be nothing to select.
- Open the file in a plain text editor to confirm digits aren’t visible.
- Zoom, change contrast, and view at high magnification to ensure just black boxes are not masking text underneath.

5) Secure transmission
- Use the requester’s secure upload portal when possible.
- If you must email, encrypt the file or password-protect the PDF and send the password through a different channel (text or phone).
- Do not send full SSN in plain email, chat, or insecure file sharing links.

6) Keep a secure record and delete insecure copies
- Keep one secure copy if needed (encrypted drive), otherwise delete temporary files and empty the recycle/trash.
- Document who you sent it to and when, if you need an audit trail.

Quick checklist before you send
- Have you confirmed the requester only needs the last 4? If yes → redact first five digits.
- Did you permanently apply redaction (not just a visual cover)? Yes/No.
- Did you remove metadata and flatten the file? Yes/No.
- Are you using a secure transmission method? Yes/No.

If you want, tell me:
- Are you working from paper or a digital file?
- Who is the recipient (HR, bank, government agency)? Different organizations sometimes require different methods.

I can walk you through step-by-step redaction instructions for your device (Windows/Mac/phone) or suggest specific tools to use.

