Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: you should avoid sending the full SSN in an unprotected scan. If the recipient only needs to verify identity, often the last four digits (SSN ending in 5678) are sufficient — but if the form explicitly requires the full SSN, use a secure submission method rather than an unsecured email attachment.

Practical guidance and safe workflow

1) Confirm the recipient’s requirement
- Ask the recipient whether a copy with only the last four digits is acceptable, or whether they require the full SSN and, if so, how they want it submitted (secure portal, encrypted PDF, over the phone, etc.).

2) If you can redact (recommended)
- Keep only the last four digits visible (e.g., “xxx-xx-5678” or “SSN ending in 5678”).
- Redact permanently so the original text cannot be recovered:
  - On paper: use a permanent black marker to fully cover the digits, then photocopy/scan the marked version (do not just cover with a post-it that could be removed).
  - Digitally: use a true redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool → Apply Redactions). After applying redactions, save as a new file. Do NOT just place a white/black box over text and save — that often leaves the underlying text intact and recoverable.
  - If you edit in Word/Pages: delete the digits, then export to PDF (don’t just hide them).

3) Prevent hidden or recoverable data
- Flatten the PDF (so text is not layered) and remove hidden data/metadata:
  - In Acrobat: “Sanitize” / “Remove Hidden Information”
  - Export as an image-based PDF, or print-to-PDF which often flattens layers.
- Check for OCR/hidden text after redaction — open the PDF and try selecting the redacted area. If you can select text under it, redaction wasn’t applied properly.

4) Use secure transfer when the full SSN is required
- Upload via the organization’s secure portal if available (preferred).
- If emailing, encrypt the file (password-protected PDF with AES-256). Share the password over a different channel (phone, SMS, or separate email).
- If you use secure email (S/MIME, PGP) confirm the recipient supports it.

5) Other safety tips
- Verify the recipient’s email address / portal is legitimate before sending PII.
- Avoid sending full SSN in plain-text in email body or attachment.
- Keep records of what you sent and how (and delete insecure copies from devices if no longer needed).
- If you must send physical mail, use tracked/certified mail services.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether last-four digits are acceptable or how they prefer to receive the full SSN.
- Walk you through redacting a file on your device (tell me whether you’re on Windows, macOS, or mobile and what software you have).

I will not send or store your SSN or any copy of it unless you explicitly ask me to send an email or create a draft — and then I will follow only your express instructions about where and how to send it. Which would you like me to do next?

