- If it asks for the last 4 digits only, supply only the last 4. Do not include the rest of the number.  
- If it explicitly requires your full SSN (e.g., many tax forms, payroll, or government forms do), you may need to provide the full number — but do so only by a secure channel.

2. If you only need to send the last 4 digits
- Redact the first five digits so the attachment shows only the last four (e.g., XXX-XX-5678).  
- Make sure the redaction is permanent (see steps below).

3. If you must send the full SSN
- Use a secure upload portal provided by the recipient, or send an encrypted attachment/password-protect the file and give the password by a different channel (phone or SMS).  
- Avoid sending full SSNs in an unencrypted email attachment or in the message body.

4. How to redact properly (so it cannot be recovered)
- Use a proper redaction tool when available (Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool). Redaction tools remove the underlying text rather than just hiding it.  
- If you don’t have a redaction tool:
  - Place a solid black rectangle over the digits (not whiteout), then “flatten” the document by printing to PDF (Print → Save as PDF) or export as a flattened image/PDF.  
  - After flattening, try selecting/copying the redacted area — if you can’t select the hidden digits, the redaction is more likely permanent.
- Remove metadata: after exporting/printing to PDF, use “Save as” or a PDF optimizer to remove metadata, or open the PDF and choose an option to remove personal info. (Some viewers have “Remove personal information” features.)
- Test: open the final file and try to search, select, or copy the redacted text to confirm it’s gone.

5. Transmission best practices
- Prefer secure portals or encrypted email. If you must use email, password-protect the file and send the password separately.  
- Do not put SSN (full or partial) directly in the email body unless instructed and the email is encrypted.
- Keep a secure local copy of the unredacted document (if needed) and delete insecure copies when no longer necessary.

6. Keep a record and limit exposure
- Only share SSN with organizations that legitimately need it. Ask the requester why and how they will protect it if you’re unsure.  
- Ask whether they accept alternate ID (last 4 digits, partial masking, or phone verification).

