Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: do not send the full SSN in an ordinary (unencrypted) email or scan unless absolutely required and the recipient provides a secure upload method. If the form only needs the last four (5678) you can usually send just that; if it needs the full number, use a secure channel and redact any copy you don’t have to share.

Recommended checklist for preparing a scanned copy safely
1. Confirm necessity
   - Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last four digits (5678) suffice.
   - If they require the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or encrypted transfer instructions.

2. If just the last four are required
   - Provide only the last four digits (5678). Redact or black out the other digits on the physical document before scanning, or redact them in the PDF so only the last four remain visible.
   - Make sure the redaction is permanent (see steps below).

3. How to redact properly (so it cannot be recovered)
   - Best: use a PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool, PDFpen, or similar). Use the tool’s “Apply Redactions” feature so the redacted text is removed from the file, not just covered.
   - If you must redact on paper: use an opaque black marker, then photocopy the page and scan the photocopy (do not scan the original with marker over it unless you then verify the blacking-out is permanent).
   - After redaction, try to select/copy text or run OCR — if any digits are selectable or hidden in metadata/OCR text, the redaction is not secure.

4. Remove metadata and hidden content
   - Save the redacted file as a new PDF and use the app’s “Remove metadata,” “Sanitize” or “Remove personal information” option.
   - Flatten the PDF (print to PDF / “Save as PDF”) to remove layers/annotations.

5. Protect the file in transit
   - Use a secure upload portal (preferred).
   - If you must email: password-protect/encrypt the PDF and send the password by a different channel (phone call or SMS).
   - Use end-to-end encrypted email or an encrypted file-transfer service (e.g., a secure company portal, SFTP, or a file-sharing service with strong access controls and link expiration).

6. Verify recipient and minimum necessary access
   - Confirm the recipient’s email address and that the person/department needs the SSN.
   - Send only the minimum information required (e.g., last four rather than full SSN whenever possible).

7. Keep a record and destroy unnecessary copies
   - Keep only the copies you need, securely store them, and securely delete/shred any unnecessary physical or electronic copies.

Legal/regulatory note
- Requirements vary by organization and jurisdiction. Many businesses accept the last four digits for identity checks; other processes (tax forms, background checks) may legally require the full SSN and will typically provide a secure upload option. When in doubt, ask the requester for their secure submission method.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the requester whether the last four (5678) are sufficient or requesting their secure upload link.
- Give step-by-step instructions for redacting in a specific app (Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, or a Windows PDF tool) — tell me which you use.

