Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — treat your SSN as highly sensitive. Only share the minimum required (often the last 4 digits) and, if you must send a scan that includes any SSN, permanently redact the rest and use a secure transmission method. Below are concrete guidelines and simple steps.

What to share
- If the requester only needs verification, ask whether “last 4 digits” are sufficient. Many organizations accept that.
- Never send the full SSN over plain email or an unsecured channel unless absolutely required and approved by the recipient’s security policy.

What to redact
- If you’re sending a document that shows the full SSN, redact everything except what the recipient explicitly requires (e.g., last 4). If they only need last 4, redact the first five digits entirely.
- Also remove or redact any other PII not necessary for the request (birthdate, driver’s license number, etc.), unless specifically needed.

How to redact correctly (do not just cover with white/black box in a PDF without flattening)
- Why: Many non‑permanent methods leave the underlying text or metadata searchable and recoverable.
- Recommended approaches:
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save as a new file. This removes underlying text and metadata.
  - For Mac Preview: Preview’s black boxes can leave underlying text. Instead, use a dedicated redaction tool (PDFpen, PDF Expert) or convert the page to an image and save a new PDF:
    - Export/Print → Save as PDF after printing the redacted view, or open the PDF in an editor and “flatten” or rasterize it so the underlying text is gone.
  - For Windows without Acrobat: Use a proper PDF redaction tool (Foxit, PDF-XChange) or open, take a high-resolution screenshot of the redacted area, then create a new PDF from the image (ensures no hidden text).
  - For images / paper originals: Manually black out the digits with a permanent marker on the paper, then scan the paper. (Black marker + scan is reliable if done thoroughly.) Alternatively, edit the scanned image and permanently paint over digits, then re-save.
- After redaction: verify by opening the redacted file and trying to select or search for the redacted digits; ensure they can’t be copied or found by search/OCR.

Secure ways to send the redacted file
- Best: Upload to the requester’s secure portal (many institutions provide an encrypted upload link).
- If no portal:
  - Encrypted email (S/MIME) or send the file as a password‑protected PDF (use strong password) and share the password separately (e.g., by phone or SMS).
  - Use a secure file-sharing service with link expiration and password protection (OneDrive/SharePoint with restricted access, Dropbox with password & expiration, or an SFTP link).
- Avoid: plain email attachments, public file links with no protections, or sending full SSN in chat/instant messengers.

Other precautions
- Confirm the recipient’s identity and why they need the SSN. If unsure, call them using a phone number from the organization’s official website.
- Keep the minimum retention — delete local copies you no longer need and empty trash/recycle bin.
- If you must send the full SSN, ask if you can provide it in person or via a secure upload/phone call instead of email.
- Keep a log of what you sent, to whom, and when, in case you need to follow up.

Quick checklist before sending a scan
1. Confirm whether last 4 digits are sufficient. If yes, redact first five digits.  
2. Redact using a tool that permanently removes the text (not just covers it).  
3. Verify redaction by trying to search/copy the redacted content.  
4. Send via secure portal or encrypted method; if using password-protected PDF, share the password separately.  
5. Delete unneeded copies and empty trash.

If you tell me:
- whether the requester accepts last 4 digits,
- how you plan to send (email, upload portal, etc.), and
- your OS (Windows/Mac) or what tools you have available (Adobe Acrobat, Preview, etc.),

I can give step‑by‑step instructions tailored to your setup.

