I can help — but first: I noticed the masked value you typed (***-**-7890) doesn't match the last-four you gave (5678). Don’t share your full SSN here. I can give safe, practical redaction and transmission advice without seeing the complete number. Do you need to send the full SSN on the scan, or will the last four digits be sufficient?

General guidance and actionable steps

1) Principle
- Only include the full SSN when absolutely required. Where possible, provide only the last 4 digits or a masked version (***-**-5678).
- Use secure channels (secure portal, encrypted transfer) whenever you must send any SSN.

2) If you only need to show the last 4 digits
- Mask the rest so the document shows only the last four: ***-**-5678 (or just “5678” if acceptable).
- Verify the recipient accepts masked SSNs before sending.

3) How to redact in a way that’s actually irreversible
- Digital PDFs: use an official Redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply). Then sanitize/remove hidden data and save a new file. This removes underlying text and metadata.
- If you only have a simple editor that draws black boxes, that may leave the underlying text in the PDF (not safe). After blocking, “flatten” the PDF (print to PDF or export as image/PDF) and verify you cannot select/copy the redacted text.
- Mac Preview: annotate with a black box, then Export or Print → Save as PDF. To be safe, Print to PDF to rasterize; confirm text cannot be selected afterward.
- Windows: use a PDF editor with a redaction feature. If not available, open the scan as an image (Paint, Paint.net), draw black over the SSN area, save as a new image or PDF (this produces a rasterized image).
- Scanned images: open the image and paint over digits, then save as JPG/PNG or convert to PDF. This is simple and effective if done before OCR.
- Paper workflow: mark the SSN with a permanent black marker, then scan the marked page. This physically removes the text from the image.
- Double-check: after redacting, try copying/selecting the redacted area (or run OCR) to confirm the digits are gone. Also remove embedded metadata (Document Properties, hidden layers, form fields).

4) Metadata and OCR layers
- PDFs created by OCR can contain a hidden text layer with the SSN. Redaction must remove both visible text and underlying text layer. Use a proper redaction tool or flatten the PDF to an image.
- Remove metadata (author, comments) and any attachments before sending.

5) Secure transmission
- Best: use the recipient’s secure upload portal.
- If you must email: password-protect the PDF with strong encryption (AES-128/256), then send the password via a separate channel (phone or SMS).
- Consider encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or secure file transfer (SFTP, secure cloud link with expiration and access controls).
- Never put SSN or masked SSN in the email subject line.

6) When full SSN is required
- Confirm the requirement and whether they have a secure submission method.
- If they insist on email, insist they provide an encrypted/upload method; otherwise, ask if you can give the SSN by phone or submit via a secure portal.

7) Recordkeeping and minimization
- Keep a copy only as long as needed; securely delete files when no longer required.
- Log who received the document and when.

Quick checklist before sending
- Do they require full SSN or only last 4? (Confirm.)
- Is there a secure portal? (Use it.)
- Has the SSN been redacted irreversibly (redaction tool or rasterized)? (Verify.)
- Is the file encrypted/password-protected if sent by email? (Yes/No.)
- Did you remove metadata and hidden layers? (Yes/No.)

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether last-four digits are acceptable or requesting a secure upload link.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting on your OS (Windows/Mac) or in Acrobat/Preview.
Tell me which option you’d like and whether the recipient requires the full SSN or only the last four.

