I can help — first a quick clarification: you wrote “***-**-7890 (last four: 5678)”. Which four-digit ending is correct (7890 or 5678)? I won’t ask for the full SSN.

Short answer
- Only share full SSNs when absolutely required. If the recipient only needs the last 4 digits, redact or remove the first five digits before scanning/sending.
- If you must send the full SSN, permanently redact parts you don’t need and send over a secure channel (encrypted email, secure portal, or password‑protected file with the password sent separately).

What to do before you send a scanned document
1. Confirm what the recipient actually needs (full SSN vs. last 4). Send only the minimum required.
2. Redact permanently (don’t just cover with a black box that can be undone).
3. Remove/clean metadata and OCR/text layers so the number isn’t hidden in the file.
4. Use a secure delivery method (secure portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file).
5. Never put SSN (full or major parts) in the subject line or file name.

How to redact properly (recommended workflows)
- PDF (best): use a dedicated redaction tool so the removed text is actually deleted.
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Remove Hidden Information (Sanitize) → Save As a new file. Reopen the saved file and search (Ctrl+F) to confirm the SSN isn’t present.
  - If you don’t have Acrobat, look for a “redact” feature in your PDF editor or use a trusted offline redaction tool. Avoid just drawing a black rectangle over text in a PDF viewer because the underlying text layer can remain.
- Image (scan or photo):
  - Open in an image editor (Paint, Preview, GIMP, Photoshop).
  - Paint a solid black rectangle over the SSN.
  - Flatten/merge layers and export as a new file (JPEG or PNG). This ensures the hidden layer is removed.
  - Remove EXIF/metadata: on Windows right‑click → Properties → Details → Remove Properties and Personal Information; on Mac use Preview → Tools → Show Inspector → remove location/metadata or use a metadata‑removal tool.
- OCR/ searchable PDFs:
  - If you scanned to a searchable PDF, the OCR text layer can contain the SSN even if you cover it visually. Use a redaction tool that removes OCR text or re-create a non-searchable image PDF after redaction (scan → edit → flatten → save).

Secure sending options
- Preferred: upload to recipient’s secure portal (bank/employer secure upload).
- Encrypted email: use S/MIME or PGP if both parties support it.
- Password‑protected file: create a password‑protected PDF or ZIP, then share the password via a different channel (phone call or SMS).
- Avoid public file‑sharing links unless the link is access‑restricted and expires.

Quick pre‑send checklist
- [ ] Only required digits remain (ideally only last 4).
- [ ] Redaction applied with a proper redaction tool or flattened image.
- [ ] Confirmed no SSN in filename or metadata.
- [ ] File saved as a new file and reopened to verify SSN is gone.
- [ ] Use secure method to send (portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file).
- [ ] Password communicated separately if used.

If you tell me:
- which last four is correct (7890 or 5678),
- what OS and program you’ll use to scan/edit (Windows, macOS, phone — and which app),
I’ll give step‑by‑step redaction instructions for that exact setup, or draft a short message you can send to the recipient describing what you redacted and how you’ll send the file.

