Short answer: If the recipient does not explicitly need your full SSN, redact everything except the last four digits (5678). If they do require the full SSN, send it only by a secure channel and confirm they really need it.

Guidance and practical steps

1) Confirm what the recipient needs
- Ask the organization which parts of the SSN they require (full SSN vs. last 4). Many businesses accept only the last four for routine verification; government forms, tax, or background checks often require the full number.
- If possible, ask for a secure upload link or instructions for submitting sensitive data.

2) Redact when you don’t need to share the full SSN
- Keep only the minimal PII necessary (e.g., last 4 digits).
- Also consider whether other fields (DOB, driver’s license number, address) can be redacted if they’re not required.

3) Secure redaction methods (recommended)
- Use a true redaction tool that permanently removes underlying text/data (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool, many reputable PDF editors).
  - Redact the number, then use the tool’s “Apply Redactions” or “Sanitize” function to remove metadata.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool:
  - Convert the scan to an image (scan settings or print-to-PDF then open and export as PNG/JPEG).
  - Use an image editor to draw a solid black box over the SSN, then save a new file. This prevents hidden text underneath.
  - After editing, create a new PDF from the edited image so the redaction is permanent.
- Avoid simply drawing a black rectangle in a Word/PDF viewer without applying/redacting—the underlying text may remain and be recoverable.

4) Physical redaction before scanning (if needed)
- Black marker the digits heavily, then photocopy/scan the marked copy (not the original). A single thin line or light stroke can sometimes be recovered; use an opaque marker and photocopy the redacted page.
- Alternatively, cut out the SSN portion and scan the rest—only if the layout permits.

5) Sending the redacted file securely
- Prefer a secure web portal/upload provided by the recipient.
- If you must email:
  - Password-protect the PDF and share the password via a different channel (phone or SMS).
  - Use encrypted email if available.
- Avoid sending SSNs in plain, unencrypted email or file-sharing links that anyone with the link can access.

6) Final checks before sending
- Verify redaction is permanent by re-opening the final file and trying to select or search for the redacted digits.
- Remove document metadata (author, hidden text) if the tool supports “sanitize.”
- Confirm the recipient’s address and that you’re sending to the correct secure location.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting this scan with a specific tool (Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, or a free tool).
- Draft a short message to the recipient asking whether they require the full SSN and their preferred secure submission method.

