Short answer: only share the full SSN if absolutely required — otherwise redact everything except the last four digits. If you must send a scan that contains the SSN, use a secure submission method and redact or protect the file so the number cannot be recovered.

Practical guidance

1. When you can avoid sending the full SSN
- Ask the recipient whether they truly need the full SSN or whether the last four digits are sufficient. Many organizations accept just the last 4.
- Prefer secure portals (employer/financial institution websites), in-person delivery, or encrypted upload rather than email.

2. If you only need to show the last four
- Redact (remove/black out) all other digits so only the last four remain visible.
- Do NOT put the full SSN in file names, email subject lines, or message bodies.

3. How to redact safely (digital)
- Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact”, macOS Preview’s redact tool in recent versions, or a reputable PDF redaction utility). These tools remove the underlying text/OCR layer permanently.
- Redact, then apply the redaction (the tool usually has a separate “Apply Redactions” step) and save a new file.
- Verify by opening the saved file and trying to select/copy the redacted area — there should be no recoverable text.
- Remove metadata: use the PDF tool’s “Remove hidden information” or export/print to a flattened PDF.
- If you must password-protect the PDF, use a strong password and send the password over a different channel (phone or SMS).

4. How to redact safely (physical paper)
- Best: request in-person or secure upload. If you must send a scan of paper:
  - Cover unwanted digits with opaque tape or black paper, then scan.
  - Alternatively, black permanent marker may still be readable in scans or via infrared — opaque tape or physically cutting out the section is safer.
  - After scanning, shred or securely destroy the paper copy.

5. If you must send the full SSN
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal or encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP, or an enterprise secure messaging service).
- Confirm how they will store and protect the SSN, how long they will keep it, and whether they will delete it afterward.
- Limit other exposure: don’t put the SSN in subject lines or filenames.

6. Final checks before sending
- Confirm recipient email address and trusted status.
- Check the file to ensure redaction is permanent.
- Remove SSN from filename and message body if not needed.
- Keep a short note recording what you sent, when, and to whom (for your records).

If you want, I can:
- Walk you through redaction steps for Windows, macOS, or a free online tool.
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether a secure upload is available (or if last four digits are sufficient).
Which would you prefer?

