Short answer: it depends. If the recipient genuinely needs your full SSN (some tax, payroll, background-check, or official government forms do), don’t redact the required portion — but only send it over a secure channel. If the full SSN is not required, share only the minimum (often just the last 4) or redact the rest before scanning and sending.

A practical checklist and guidance

1) Confirm what’s required
- Ask the requestor whether they really need the full SSN or whether the last four digits will suffice.
- If they insist on a full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (secure portal, encrypted email, or fax) before sending.

2) If you must redact, redact permanently (don’t just hide)
- Use a true redaction tool that removes the underlying text/pixels and any OCR/searchable text, not just a black box overlay.
- After redaction, flatten the document and save as a new file.

3) Step-by-step (digital redaction — recommended)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → File → Save As (use a new filename). Verify you cannot select/copy the redacted numbers.
- PDF-XChange Editor or similar: use the built-in Redact feature (not markup/annotation).
- After redaction, remove metadata: Acrobat: File → Properties → Additional Metadata → Remove Hidden Information, or use ExifTool to strip metadata.
- Verify: try selecting/copying the redacted area or run a text search for the SSN pattern to ensure it’s gone.

4) If you only have image editors or are scanning paper
- On paper, use a permanent black marker to cover the digits, then scan — but verify the covered digits are unreadable in the scanned file. Best is to use software redaction after scanning.
- Better: scan, then open the scan in an editor (GIMP, Photoshop) and permanently erase pixels where needed; save and export to PDF (flatten).

5) Secure transmission
- Preferred: upload to the recipient’s secure portal (HR/finance portals, encrypted file share).
- If emailing: attach a password-protected PDF (use strong password) and send the password via a different channel (text or phone).
- Better yet: use end-to-end encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) if available.
- Avoid sending unencrypted attachments with full SSN over regular email.

6) Other protections to check
- Remove filename details that include your SSN.
- Don’t include extra personal identifiers unless necessary (DOB, account numbers) — only what is required.
- Keep a copy only if you must; otherwise securely delete/shred.

7) Is sharing last 4 safe?
- The last four digits are commonly used for ID verification and are lower-risk than the full SSN, but they are still personally identifying in combination with other data. Use them only when requested and when the recipient is verified.

Quick template you can send to the requester
"Do you need the full SSN for this form, or will the last four digits suffice? If you need the full SSN, please provide a secure upload link or confirm you accept an encrypted/ password‑protected PDF; I will send the password separately."

Note: I noticed you wrote two different “last four” values (***-**-7890 and last four: 5678). Which is correct? (Don’t post the full SSN here.)

If you want, I can:
- Draft a message to request a secure upload option.
- Walk you step-by-step through redaction with a specific tool (tell me whether you use Acrobat, Preview/Mac, Windows, or only image editors).

